   "text": {
    "L1": "Stars are huge, very hot balls of glowing gas that make their own light.",
    "L2": "The Sun is the star nearest to us, while other stars look tiny because they are extremely far away.",
    "L3": "A constellation is a group of stars forming a pattern as seen from Earth, such as Saptarishi or Orion.",
    "L4": "Stars are in the sky during the day too, but scattered sunlight makes the sky too bright to see them.",
    "L5": "Stars seem to move from east to west because Earth spins from west to east.",
    "L6": "Saptarishi's two pointer stars help us find the Pole Star, which shows the direction north.",
    "L7": "A shooting star is not a star. It is a small piece of rock or dust burning in Earth's air.",
    "L8": "Now choose the star that helps travellers find north."
   },
